cancer is the second leading cause of  death accounting for nearly 21.7 percent  of all deaths per year in the United  States with cancer being so frequent and  deadly you'd want the most effective  treatments if you ever got diagnosed  ultimately survival comes down to the  type of cancer and the degree of its  aggressiveness surgery and radiation are  very effective tools as they do cause  the least harm to the patient and have  good success rates but surgery and  radiation only work in specific  situations like if the tumor is found  early and hasn't spread but what if the  cancer metastasizes you can't surgically  remove mobile cancer cells and radiation  can't pinpoint every new potential  growth this is where chemotherapy comes  in chemotherapy isn't an actual drug but  an umbrella term for drugs that target  cancer like cells the chemo drugs are  injected into the patient's bloodstream  and circulate throughout the body in  hopes that it comes in contact with  tumors growth in muscles and organs but  how do chemotherapy drugs actually kill  cancer cells simple they target the  cell's DNA if the cell's DNA is damaged  or the replication process that copies  DNA is damaged the cell will die this is  because cells have a natural safety  mechanism that causes it to undergo a  puto --ss or cell suicide when DNA is  negatively affected this is to prevent  mutated or damaged DNA from replicating  in proliferating a bad cell line  so basically chemo breaks the DNA and  the cell commits suicide to stop the DNA  from making more broken DNA chemotherapy  drugs affect DNA in two ways either by  directly attacking the DNA itself or  indirectly by inhibiting enzymes that  replicate the DNA for example some  chemotherapy drugs attack DNA itself by  cross-linking neighboring nucleotides to  each other preventing it from being  replicated properly cell mechanisms see  that the DNA can't replicate and it  initiates cell suicide other chemo drugs  will inhibit an enzyme called  topoisomerase which unwinds strands of  DNA so it can be properly replicated  with this enzyme halted replication can  complete and the cell activates its  suicide mechanism a more widely used  class of chemo drugs are called mitotic  inhibitors these drugs attacks  cells that are a particular phase of  their cell cycle in this case the cells  are in mitosis mitosis prepares the cell  to undergo cell division into two new  cells once in mitosis the mitotic  inhibitors block enzymes that complete  the cycle locking the cell in mitosis  causing it to activate a Patou --ss  these drugs are very useful in targeting  cancer cells because cancer cells have  abnormally high rates of growth and are  therefore more likely to be in mitosis  than a regular slower growing cell  cancers like leukemia which are  particularly aggressive and growth are  heavily targeted by mitotic inhibitors  unfortunately cancer cells aren't the  only fast-growing cells in the body  epithelial cells in the intestine skin  cells and cells involved in hair growth  have very high growth rates and are  targeted by mitotic inhibitors this  explains why cancer patients lose their  hair get skin rashes and have bouts of  vomiting from excessive cell death now  you may think that mitotic inhibitors  are a cure-all for cancer it's far from  that one reason is that not all cancers  grow fast this is a setback for mitotic  inhibitor drugs because if the cells  grow slow that means the cell will less  likely be in mitosis and therefore are  resistant to mitotic inhibitors making  these treatments more harmful than good  this is why cancer is often dealt with a  multi treatment approach you'd be crazy  to solely rely on chemo or surgery alone  often multiple methods are used for  example once the tumor is found and  removed its location is then radiate  additionally chemotherapy may also be  used in case of potential metastasis  [Music]
 

